% ============================================================================
% solfege-alt-name — for the movable-do solfège syllables the SAME cited
% Wikipedia span also gives an alternate spelling or name for, that
% alternate (adj-facts-stdlib, MUSIC).
% ============================================================================
% A sibling to the already-shipped `solfege.adj` (`solfege_degree(syllable,
% degree)`, ONE scale-degree number per syllable). That table's own `source`
% field already quotes, VERBATIM, an alternate spelling or name for two of
% the seven syllables — but the degree-only schema had no room for it:
%
%   do → doh
%     "do (spelt doh in tonic sol-fa)"
%
%   ti → si
%     "ti (or si)"
%
% Recall is a binding query:
%
%     ? solfege_alt_name(do, $Alt)   % doh
%     ? solfege_alt_name(ti, $Alt)   % si
%
% This is a native `table` (ADJ-TABLES RS-5, an ordinary two-column EXACT
% lookup): each `row` lowers to a ground relation `solfege_alt_name(syllable,
% alt_name)` carrying the citation, so the lookup above is the ordinary SLD
% binding query — the engine returns the alternate WITH its source, on the
% CPU, with zero model calls, and abstains on the other five syllables (re,
% mi, fa, sol, la), since the SAME cited span states no alternate spelling
% or name for any of them — the same honest-abstention-by-simple-absence
% discipline `coin-penny-discontinued.adj` uses for every coin but the
% penny. The query also runs BACKWARD — bind an alternate and recall which
% syllable it names:
%
%     ? solfege_alt_name($Syllable, si)   % ti
%
% Provenance: reproduces, byte-for-byte, the SAME Wikipedia "Solfège" span
% already quoted inside `solfege.adj`'s own `source` field — no new
% WebFetch, only a different schema decoding the alternate-name half of
% that already-verified quote. `trust consensus` — the same tier
% `solfege.adj` already carries (an encyclopedia is a secondary reference).
% (See ../README.md; feedback_nothing_human_authored,
% feedback_adj_only_shipped_artifacts.)
% ============================================================================

table solfege_alt_name {
    columns syllable, alt_name

    row (do, doh)   % Wikipedia Solfège: "do (spelt doh in tonic sol-fa)"
    row (ti, si)    % Wikipedia Solfège: "ti (or si)"

    source "The tonic sol-fa method popularised the seven syllables commonly used in English-speaking countries: do (spelt doh in tonic sol-fa), re, mi, fa, so(l), la, and ti (or si)"
    locator "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Solf%C3%A8ge"
    trust consensus
}
